# Using generic types

## Supply type arguments

```java
List<String>     names;   // E = String
Map<Long, User>  users;    // K = Long, V = User
```

Every method specialises — `add` demands a `String`, `get` returns a `User`. No casts.

## The diamond — don't repeat yourself

```java
Map<String, List<Integer>> m = new HashMap<>();   // <> infers from the left
var names = new ArrayList<String>();               // var → type on the RIGHT
```

## Nesting composes

```java
Map<String, List<Order>> ordersByCustomer;   // read inside-out
```

Deeply nested? → introduce a **record** for readability.

## Avoid raw types

```java
List raw = new ArrayList();   // "unchecked" — type-checking OFF
raw.add(42);                  // no error → safety lost
```

Mean "list of anything"? → `List<?>` (wildcard, §06), **not** raw.
